      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coopersburg was originally called Freyburg, named after tavern owner Joseph Frey, before being renamed in 1832 to honor Judge Peter Cooper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coopersburg%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Coopersburg Historical Society, founded in 1914, preserves artifacts and stories that trace the borough’s 250‑year heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.coopersburghistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Railroad tracks arrived in 1856, turning Coopersburg into a bustling hub for carriage makers and textile mills in the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.coopersburghistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The borough hall houses the Coopersburg Historical Society museum, where visitors can explore the town’s unique blend of historic charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.findglocal.com/US/Coopersburg/125007020911137/Coopersburg-Historical-Society%2C-Inc.",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Known as the “Town of Possibilities” since 1914, Coopersburg celebrates its heritage with well‑preserved structures that echo over two centuries of American life.",
